{
  "dom_id": "step-q106724",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=$",
  "worked_solution": "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-3)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\\infty \n$$",
